                                                Types of Operating Systems


                                                    Operating System
                                                            │
                                        ┌───────────────────┼────────────────────┐
                                        │                   │                    │
                                       Job Handling      Processor Support    Timing
                                        │                   │                    │
                                     Batch OS         Multiprocessing      Real-Time OS
                                    Time Sharing
                                        │
                                   Distributed
                                      Network
                                     Clustered
                                         │
                                 Mobile / Embedded

1. Batch OS — 
Jobs are grouped and processed in batches without user interaction in between. Once one job finishes, the next starts
automatically. Used in early mainframes for payroll, statements, etc. Problem: one stuck job blocks the whole batch.

2. Time-Sharing (Multitasking) OS —
CPU time is sliced into small quanta and rapidly switched between programs, so multiple users/apps feel like they're 
running simultaneously. This is what Windows, Linux, and macOS do every day on your desktop.

3. Multiprocessing OS —
Multiple CPUs work in parallel within one system.

Symmetric (SMP): any CPU can run any task.
Asymmetric (AMP): a master CPU assigns work to slave CPUs.
Gives higher throughput and fault tolerance.

4. Distributed OS —
Independent machines connected over a network behave as a single logical system. Resources live on different 
physical nodes but appear unified to the user. Highly scalable and resilient to node failure.

5. Network OS (NOS) —
Runs on a server to share resources (files, printers) across client machines. Unlike distributed OS, users know 
exactly which machine holds which resource — no transparency illusion.

6. Real-Time OS (RTOS) —
Tasks must complete within strict deadlines.

Hard real-time: missing a deadline is catastrophic (airbags, pacemakers, missile guidance).
Soft real-time: missing a deadline just degrades quality (video streaming, gaming).
Predictability matters more than raw speed.

7. Embedded OS — 
Built into a single-purpose device with limited resources (smart TVs, ATMs, washing machines, car infotainment). 
Efficient and minimal by design.

8. Mobile OS — Optimized for touch input, battery life, and app sandboxing. Android and iOS are the dominant examples.

9. Clustered OS — Multiple tightly-linked nodes act as one system for high availability. If one node fails, another
takes over (failover) — common in database and web server clusters.
